## A waiting room in Frankfurt

Week 26. An EU healthcare team — call them a mid-size diagnostic imaging group with deployments in Germany, Austria, and the Netherlands — sat down with their legal counsel for what was supposed to be a thirty-minute compliance sign-off. They had spent five months building a triage co-pilot on a US-hosted frontier model. Eval scores were good. The radiologists liked it. Procurement was lined up.

Counsel asked one question: where does the inference happen? The answer was a US region. The follow-up: where does the patient data live, and under what processing agreement does it cross? The room went quiet. Six months of work hit a wall not because the model was wrong, but because the model was unreachable from where the data was legally allowed to sit.

The lesson the team carried out: portability is not a property of the weights. It is a property of the surface you can deploy them on, in the jurisdiction you actually operate in.

## The high-school version

Think of a model the way you think of a chef. A great chef can cook anywhere — in theory. But if the kitchen has to be in Frankfurt, and the chef is contractually only allowed to cook in California, and the ingredients are not allowed on a plane, then the chef's skill stops mattering. What matters is who is allowed to cook where, with whose ingredients, under whose rules.

Model portability is the question of how easily you can move a model — or swap it for another — without breaking your product. The answer is "very easily, until it isn't," and the "isn't" is almost always about geography, contracts, or hardware, not about machine learning.

## The technical version

When engineers talk about portability they usually mean something narrow: can I swap GPT-4-class for Claude-class behind an OpenAI-compatible API and keep my prompts, evals, and tools working? For that flavor of portability, the answer is mostly yes — and getting better — because the industry has converged on a small number of interface shapes.

But portability has at least four distinct axes, and most teams only check the first:

- **Interface portability.** Same request/response shape, same tool-calling protocol, same streaming semantics.
- **Behavioral portability.** Same prompt produces equivalent outputs at acceptable variance. This is where most swaps quietly fail.
- **Deployment portability.** Can the weights run in your VPC, your region, your air-gapped enclave, your on-prem GPU?
- **Legal portability.** Does your data processing agreement, your sub-processor list, your residency commitment, and your sectoral regulation permit inference in the place the model is reachable from?

Interface portability is solved. Behavioral portability is partially solved by good evals. Deployment and legal portability are where projects die.

## The formal frame

The research literature treats portability mostly as a distillation or transfer problem — Hinton et al.'s knowledge distillation (2015) is the canonical reference for compressing a teacher model's behavior into a smaller student that fits a different deployment envelope. More recent work on LoRA adapters (Hu et al., 2021) extends the same logic: keep the base, swap the cap.

What the literature does not formalize, because it is a deployment concern rather than a modeling concern, is the constraint topology of where a given set of weights is *permitted* to execute. That is a graph problem over jurisdictions, contracts, and certifications, and it dominates the optimization long before perplexity does.

## The trade-offs

Every step you take toward portability costs you something at the top of the stack. Building against a least-common-denominator interface means giving up provider-specific features — extended context windows, native caching, structured output modes, proprietary tool-calling extensions — that often deliver the largest single quality jump in the system. Behavioral portability costs you eval surface area: every model you support is another matrix cell to maintain.

Deployment portability is the most expensive. The moment you commit to "must run in-region, in our VPC, possibly air-gapped," you cut the candidate model list by an order of magnitude and you take on the operational burden of running inference yourself. You lose the ability to ride the frontier — by the time a new model is deployable in your environment, the hosted version has been in production for six months elsewhere.

The decision lever: portability is a tax you pay on quality and velocity in exchange for not getting trapped. Pay it deliberately, not by default.

## The scar

Back to the imaging team. Their actual failure was not the legal meeting — it was the architectural decision in week 2, when someone asked "should we plan for in-region deployment?" and someone else said "let's get the prototype working first and figure out hosting later." That decision compounded for twenty-four weeks. The prompts grew dependent on a 200K context window the in-region candidates did not offer. The tool-calling glue assumed a function-calling dialect only the original provider shipped. The evals were tuned to the original model's quirks.

When the team finally tried to port to a Frankfurt-hosted open-weights model that satisfied counsel, they did not just swap an API key. They rewrote the prompt layer, rebuilt the tool dispatcher, retuned the eval thresholds, and accepted a measurable quality drop the radiologists noticed within a week. The lesson was not "we picked the wrong model." It was "we let the unreachable model define the shape of our system."

## Prototype assignment

In an afternoon: take any existing prompt-and-tool agent you have running on a hosted frontier model. Stand up an open-weights model of roughly one tier below — Llama, Mistral, Qwen, dealer's choice — on a local GPU or a region-locked endpoint. Run your existing eval suite against both. Do not tune. Record the delta. The number you get is your portability tax for that system, today. Re-run it every quarter. Watch it shrink.

## Spirit

The determinism-ladder thesis says: push work down the stack, swap autonomy for determinism wherever you can. Model selection is the top rung — the place with the most autonomy and the least determinism. Portability is what keeps that rung from becoming load-bearing in a way you cannot escape. A system that assumes one specific model is a system that has handed a vendor, a jurisdiction, and a regulator each a veto over your roadmap. Engineer for the swap, even if you never make it.